"""Chapter 20 deck notes, version 4.0.1 (SEN0414, Lineage13; over 4.0.0 only the note of the AT&T story changes): the notes the reader gets with the slides, one for every slide, written in full sentences for a student who reads the deck alone.
Each note explains the short wording of its slide, names what the run printed, and points to a related slide; none gives a direction to a presenter and none uses an everyday comparison.
RESULT_SAYS holds the first result balloon of every code slide (what the run showed); the second balloon is TAKEAWAY of the content file. Slide numbers are those of PLAN."""
__version__ = "4.0.1"

NOTES = {
"title": "This chapter of the book is about sending email, texts and push notifications from a program. It has nineteen examples, and all of them run on the chapter page in the order of this deck, with no account and no network. Google's service is replaced by a stand-in object, and the mail server and the notification server run on the same machine; every slide that does this says so. Parts 1 and 2 cover email, part 3 covers texts, part 4 covers push notifications and part 5 combines them in three practice programs. Slide 2 shows the route through the five parts.",
"route": "The chapter has five parts. Part 1 is the Gmail API, the easy way to send and read mail today. Part 2 is mail as a protocol with smtplib, which shows what really travels to a server. Part 3 covers texts sent through email gateways. Part 4 covers push notifications with ntfy, which replaced the gateways for many people. Part 5 has three practice programs. The table gives the slides of each part and how many examples it holds, and the chapter-code tab of the page lists the same examples in the same order. Slide 3 states the two questions that frame the chapter.",
"questions": "Two questions frame the chapter. The first asks how a program sends a message to a person without lying about who it is from and without sending it to the wrong people. The second asks how it does so without putting a secret in the wrong place, such as a password in a file, a topic name in a public list, or a command that a stranger can choose. The summary on slide 41 answers the first, and the practice questions on slide 40 let you check yourself on the second. Part 1 starts on slide 4.",
"divider1": "Part 1 is about the Gmail API through the ezgmail module. A program needs two files before it can send anything: the credentials that Google issues once, and the token that the module saves after the first sign-in. Then the part sends, reads, searches, replies and downloads attachments. Every example uses a stand-in for Google's service, so the run shows exactly what the module hands over, without touching a real mailbox. The table lists the slides of the part. Slide 5 explains its concepts in plain words.",
"divider2": "Part 2 goes under the hood. A mail is a set of headers and a body, built with the email package and sent with smtplib by short commands to a server. The part shows how accents are written in a header, why a line break in a header is refused, how STARTTLS encrypts the conversation, how a server refuses a recipient, and how the envelope differs from the headers. That last difference explains how a blind copy works. The table lists the slides of the part. Slide 13 explains its concepts in plain words.",
"divider3": "Part 3 covers texts sent by email. A carrier can turn mail sent to a special address into a text on a phone, and the book lists these addresses in a table. The part builds such an address and counts how many text messages a given text needs. A story then shows that the carrier can close this door, and a table shows what became of the addresses of the book. The table lists the slides of the part. Slide 23 explains the concepts in plain words.",
"divider4": "Part 4 is about push notifications with ntfy. One web request to a topic sends a message, and every phone that subscribes to the topic shows it. In the simple case there is no account, so the name of the topic is the only secret. The part publishes a message, adds a title and a priority in headers, reads the stream that comes back and shows why a short topic name is a weak password. The table lists the slides of the part. Slide 30 explains the concepts in plain words.",
"divider5": "Part 5 has three small programs that put the earlier parts together. The unsubscriber finds the safe link in a mail. The umbrella reminder reads a forecast and decides by the chance of rain. The third program obeys commands in mail, with a password and a list of allowed commands. After them come the practice questions, the summary and the links. Each program uses calls from earlier parts. The table lists the slides of the part. Slide 36 explains the concepts of the three programs in plain words.",
"A1": "The Gmail API needs two files. credentials.json is issued by Google once, when you enable the API for your project. token.json is written by the module after the first sign-in in a browser, so that later runs need none. The table gives each concept of the part its meaning: ezgmail wraps the API in short calls, send() sends, searching returns threads, and an attachment arrives with a name that the sender chose. Slide 6 tells the story of IMAP, and slide 7 runs the first example.",
"A2": "A mail has headers, which the reader sees, and an envelope, which the server delivers by. smtplib sends both, and the two can differ. The table gives the concepts of the part their meanings: smtplib talks to a server, imaplib reads a mailbox and leaves the mail there, logging records the conversation, and datetime writes the Date header. Slide 14 tells where the @ came from, slide 15 tells about the protocol itself, and slide 16 builds a message with the email package.",
"A3": "A gateway is an address made of a phone number and the carrier's mail domain. Mail sent to it arrives as a text message on the phone. The domain belongs to the carrier, so the carrier can switch the gateway off without asking you. The table gives the two concepts of the part their meanings, and the book's Table 20-1 lists the domains as they stood when it was written. Slide 24 tells the story of the first text message, slide 26 counts how many texts a message needs, and slide 28 shows what became of the list.",
"A4": "A push notification is one web request to a topic. The body of the request is the message, and the title, the priority and the tags travel as headers, which must be Latin-1. Reading is a GET request that returns one JSON object on each line. The table gives each concept of the part its meaning, including the requests module that sends the request and the json module that reads the answer. Anyone who knows the topic name can read and write it, which slide 34 turns into a rule. Slide 31 runs the first example.",
"A5": "The three practice programs combine the earlier parts. The unsubscriber finds the link that stops a mailing and opens it only for senders you know. The umbrella reminder reads a forecast and decides by the number for the chance of rain. The mail-controlled program reads mail, checks a password and a list of allowed commands, and starts only an allowed command. The table gives the concepts of the part their meanings, including Beautiful Soup, webbrowser, subprocess and the torrent client of the book. Slide 37 runs the first program.",
"setup": "The first printed line is the message that the credentials file cannot be found, and the second line shows the scope followed by False, which says that nobody is signed in. Before a program can use Gmail it needs two files: credentials.json, downloaded once after the API is enabled, and token.json, which the module creates after the first sign-in in a browser. Here the folder is empty, so init() stops with an exception that names the missing file, which is the most common first error. The scope asks for full access to the mailbox. Slide 8 sends the first message.",
"send": "The first printed line shows the recipient, the sender and the subject, and the second line shows the body: the result is the mail that was typed. ezgmail.send() takes the recipient, the subject and the body, and nothing more is needed. A stand-in object replaces Google's service and remembers its last call, so the example decodes what ezgmail handed to it. Gmail wants the whole message as one base64 text, and decoding it gives back an ordinary email. The From line is the account that the module signed in with. No message was sent to anyone. Slide 9 reads and searches the mailbox.",
"read": "The first printed line shows the sender, the subject and the body of the one message found. The second line is an empty list, and the third line shows the search text that reached the service. Searching returns threads, and each thread holds messages whose sender, subject and body can be read as plain attributes. The search text is passed to Gmail unchanged, so everything typed in the Gmail search box works here, for example from:, has:attachment or a word. The stand-in service builds its answer the way Google's API does, with headers as pairs of names and values and the body as base64. Slide 10 replies to a message.",
"reply": "The first printed line shows the thread number T1, the sender and the subject of the original mail, and the second line shows the reply text. Replying is a send with two things done for you: the answer goes to the sender of the mail, and it is filed in the same thread, which is why the thread number is the one of the original. The stand-in service lets the example read what would have been sent. The module does not write a Re: prefix, so check how replies look in a real mailbox before relying on them. Slide 11 treats attachments and why their names cannot be trusted.",
"download": "The first printed list holds one saved name, which starts with two dots and a slash, so the file left the folder that was chosen. The second list is empty, and the last line shows that the inbox folder holds no file while the file landed one level up. A mail can carry an attachment, and the sender chooses its name. This is a path traversal, and it is a real finding of this study. The lesson is general: a name received from outside is data, not a safe file name, so clean it before writing a file. Slide 12 starts part 2, with mail as a protocol.",
"build": "The first printed list shows five content types: multipart/mixed, multipart/alternative, text/plain, text/html and application/octet-stream. The email package builds a message as a tree. The top is a multipart mixed container; inside it a multipart alternative holds the plain text and the HTML version of the same body, and the attachment sits beside it. walk() visits every part in that order. The second result prints poster.bin, the name of the attachment found by iter_attachments, and the last shows that get_body with HTML preferred answers with the HTML part. Building mail with this class is safer than gluing strings together. Slide 17 shows its headers.",
"headers": "The first printed line shows an accented subject and a sender with a name. The second line shows the subject written as an encoded word, because headers are plain ASCII on the wire. The third line shows that parseaddr split the name from the address. The last line shows that a value with a line break is refused with an error. If such a value were accepted, a second header could be added, for example a hidden copy to a stranger. Never build headers by gluing together text that came from outside. Slide 18 shows how the connection to the server is encrypted.",
"starttls": "The three printed lines are the answer 250 to the greeting, the code 220 for go ahead, and SSLSocket, which shows the connection is now wrapped in TLS. A connection to a mail server begins in plain text, and STARTTLS is the request to switch to an encrypted one. The client says hello, the server lists what it can do and offers STARTTLS, the client asks for it, and 220 says go ahead. From then on everything is encrypted. The client also checks the certificate, which here comes from a one-day test certificate made with openssl. Never turn that check off to make an error go away. Slide 19 shows refused recipients.",
"refusals": "The two printed lines are the dictionary of refused addresses, first returned by sendmail and then carried by the exception: the address x@refused.example with the code 550. A mail server can refuse a recipient with a code, and here a stand-in server refuses one domain. If only some recipients are refused, sendmail still delivers to the others and returns a dictionary of the refused ones, so check it. If all are refused, smtplib raises SMTPRecipientsRefused, which carries the same dictionary. If no server is listening, a connection error comes before any mail exists. None of the three is a silent success. Slide 20 shows how the envelope differs from the headers.",
"envelope": "The first printed line shows the sender and the two addresses the server delivered to, Jon and the boss, although the header names only Jon. The second line says that the boss is not in the text. Two lists of recipients exist in every mail: the headers say who the mail is for, and the envelope says who the server delivers it to. The text the receiver sees contains no sign of the second person, and that is how a blind copy works. It also explains why the From header is not proof of who sent a mail, because the sender writes both lists and nobody checks them. Slide 21 tells the story of the first spam.",
"gateway": "The first printed line is the gateway address, the phone number joined to the carrier's domain. The second line shows 4 carriers on 3 domains, with Verizon and Xfinity Mobile sharing one, and the third line shows 517 days since AT&T closed its gateway. Building an address is a plain string join. The two limits are that two carriers can share one domain, so the carrier cannot be found from the address, and that the addresses can be switched off: AT&T ended its email-to-text on 17 June 2025. The chapter itself warns that what works today may not work tomorrow. Slide 26 counts how many texts a message needs.",
"segments": "Each printed line shows the coding, the character count and the number of parts. A message of 13 characters in the seven-bit alphabet is one part. A message of 161 characters needs two parts. A message of 20 characters in the seven-bit alphabet is one part, and the same length with the Turkish dotless i moves to UCS-2, a sixteen-bit coding, and is still one part. In the seven-bit alphabet one message holds 160 characters, and a longer one is cut into parts of 153. In UCS-2 one message holds 70 characters, or 67 per part. Count in the coding the phone uses, not the characters Python shows. Slide 27 tells how a gateway was closed.",
"ntfyPub": "The printed lines show the request as the recorder received it: the method POST and the path with the topic name, then the body Hello, world! with its length of 13, and a last line with None. Publishing a notification with ntfy is one web request. The topic is the path of the address, and the message is the body. There is no account and no key in this simple case. A small recorder on this machine stands in for ntfy.sh and keeps what it receives. Nothing else identifies the receiver, so anyone who knows the topic name can read and write it, which slide 34 turns into a rule. Slide 32 adds a title and a priority.",
"ntfyHdr": "The first printed line is the dictionary of three headers as the recorder received them: the title, the tags and the priority. The last line is an error name: a UnicodeEncodeError in a header. The title, the priority and the tags of a notification are sent as headers, and the message stays in the body. Headers must be written in Latin-1, so a dotless i raises the error before anything is sent. If a title needs such characters, put them in the body or encode them as the service documents. A header is the wrong place for free text. Slide 33 reads the topic.",
"ntfyPoll": "The first printed line is the list of kinds: an open and two messages. The next two lines are the clock times of the two messages. Receiving works the other way: a request to the topic returns what it holds as one JSON object on each line. The answer here is typed into the program, so the run never changes. Each line is parsed with json.loads. The first event is not a message but an open, which tells that the stream began, so only the events of kind message are kept. The time is epoch seconds, as in chapter 19, and datetime turns it into a clock time. Slide 34 shows why the topic name is a password.",
"secrecy": "The four printed lines are the strength of three topic names and the size of a message. A name of 18 mixed letters and digits is at most 107.2 bits, a guessable 8-letter name is 47.6 bits, and a name made by the secrets module has 144 bits from the system's random source. The last line shows that twenty characters with one accented letter take twenty-one bytes. With ntfy the topic name is the password, and a name of about 48 bits can be guessed by a program. The size limit counts bytes, not characters. Slide 35 starts part 5, with the practice programs.",
"unsub": "The first printed list holds the link that was found in the text, /u?id=1. The second list holds the two addresses of the List-Unsubscribe header: a mail address and a web address. The practice program finds the link that unsubscribes you. The book uses Beautiful Soup, and here the parser of the standard library does the same: it remembers the address of each link and keeps it when the text of the link says unsubscribe. Well-made mail also carries the List-Unsubscribe header, which is the safe way to find the address. Nothing is opened here. Only unsubscribe from senders you know, because for a spammer a click proves that your address is alive. Slide 38 reads a forecast.",
"umbrella": "The two printed lines are the decisions: Tonight has a chance of 60 and takes an umbrella, and Friday has a chance of 0 and does not. The forecast is typed in as a list of periods in the shape that a weather service returns, so the run never changes. The word rain does not appear in the text of the period Showers And Thunderstorms, so searching for the word fails, while the chance of precipitation says sixty. A period with no chance has the value None, which is treated as zero before comparing. Decide by the number, with a rule that can be stated: fifty or more means take an umbrella. Slide 39 shows the last program.",
"control": "The three printed lines are the outcomes: wrong password, not allowed, and ran hello with the output printed. The book's last program starts a download when it receives a mail, which is the most dangerous idea of the chapter, because whoever can send mail to the program can make it run things. The safe form has two checks. The password is compared in constant time, and the command must be on a short list of allowed commands, each stored as a list of arguments. A wrong password stops first, a command that is not on the list stops second, and only an allowed command runs. Never pass text from a mail to a shell. Slide 40 lists the practice questions.",
"compare_gateways": "The table gathers what the study found about the gateway domains of the book's table, by a DNS query for their mail servers. The two domains of AT&T and the text address of Cricket had none, two of Sprint's answered that they do not exist, one of Boost failed, and twelve had mail servers. A missing mail server is a symptom, not proof, so the takeaway says so. The carriers' own notices are the evidence: AT&T ended its service on 17 June 2025, and Verizon expects its shutdown of vtext.com and vzwpix.com to be complete by 31 March 2027. Slide 29 starts part 4, with push notifications.",
"practice": "These are the practice questions, and each one points to the part where the idea was shown. The first is about the two files of the Gmail module, slide 7. The second is about the envelope and the headers, slide 20. The third is about counting texts, slide 26. The fourth is about the topic name as a password, slide 34. The fifth is about the two checks of the program that obeys mail, slide 39. Predict an answer first, then run what you can on the chapter page. Slide 41 summarises the chapter.",
"summary": "Five sentences carry the chapter. The Gmail API needs two files, and a received file name is data, not a safe file name. A mail is headers and a body, and the envelope decides who gets it. A text is counted in the coding the phone uses, and its gateway can be switched off. A notification topic is a password, so give it enough randomness. A program that obeys mail needs a password and a list of allowed commands. The first three points answer the first question of slide 3, and the last two the second. The chart counts the examples of each part. Slide 42 lists where to go next.",
"resources": "These fifteen links are where to go next. They include the chapter of the book, the documentation of the modules, the protocol and the service pages, and the pages behind the stories. The Gmail guide, the ntfy documentation and the RFC are primary sources, and the Wikipedia pages are good for the history of mail and texts, told on slides 6, 14, 15, 21 and 24. Every link was opened and answered on 12 November 2026. There is no video and no community link yet, which the lineage records as a gap. Choose one link that matches your weakest part and read it this week.",
}

STORY_NOTES = {
"imap": "In 1986 Mark Crispin at Stanford designed IMAP, a protocol that keeps the mail on the server. The widely used POP simply fetched a mailbox. With IMAP several programs can share one mailbox, because nothing leaves the server until a client deletes it. The first client ran on a Xerox Lisp machine against a TOPS-20 server, and the improved IMAP2 became RFC 1064 in 1988 and RFC 1176 in 1990. The picture is a Lisp machine of the kind in the story. The lesson: IMAP leaves mail on the server, so many programs can share one mailbox. Slide 7 sets up the Gmail API.",
"tomlinson": "Ray Tomlinson at Bolt, Beranek and Newman wrote the first email program that could send mail between users on different hosts of the ARPANET, in 1971. Before that, mail could reach only people on the same computer. He needed a sign to separate the user name from the machine name, and he chose the @ because nobody used it in user names or in TENEX programs. The picture is a map of the ARPANET. The lesson: every address your program builds still has Tomlinson's @ in it. Slide 15 tells the story of the protocol itself.",
"rfc821": "In August 1982 Jonathan Postel of the Information Sciences Institute published RFC 821, which defined the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol. The page of the RFC now says it is obsolete, replaced by RFC 2821 and then by RFC 5321 of October 2008. Yet smtplib still speaks the same conversation: a greeting, then MAIL FROM and RCPT TO. The picture is a diagram of the SMTP transfer model. The lesson: mail is an old protocol, so trust nothing in it that is not checked. Slide 16 builds a message with the email package.",
"spam": "In 1978 Gary Thuerk sent the first known spam, an advertisement for a presentation of the Digital Equipment Corporation, over the ARPANET, where commercial mail was forbidden. Counts of recipients differ between sources, so the slide says several hundred. He was reprimanded. Only slightly more than half of the intended people received it, because of software limits. The picture shows a machine of the family DEC made. The lesson: volume is judged by the receiver, so send little and say who you are. Slide 22 starts part 3, on texts by email.",
"sms": "On 3 December 1992 Neil Papworth, an engineer of Sema Group, sent the first text, Merry Christmas, from a computer to Richard Jarvis's phone on the Vodafone network in the United Kingdom. The length was limited so that a message fits the signalling formats that already existed, first 128 bytes and then 160 characters of seven bits. The picture is a phone showing a text message. The lesson: count the characters in the coding the phone uses, not in Python. Slide 25 builds a gateway address.",
"att": "AT&T told its customers that on 17 June 2025 it shut down its email-to-text and text-to-email service and gave no reason. Commentators suggest that it was old, open to abuse and free, but these are their opinions. A DNS query in this study found no mail servers for its two gateway domains. The picture is a photograph of an AT&T store in Asheville, North Carolina, taken in May 2025 by Harrison Keely under a CC BY licence; it shows a shop front, not the service itself. The lesson: a gateway the carrier owns can be switched off without asking you. Slide 28 shows what became of the book's gateway table.",
}

